**Theorem 3.1 (N1: unique optimizing line measure).** For every $C_{\rm end}<\lambda<C_{\rm sat}$ there is exactly one optimizing measure on failure lines. It has one positive-$k$ line and one or two negative-$k$ lines, hence at most three lines. It has no zero-$k$ or axis contact. Up to antipodes and proportional splitting its weights are unique. $\Phi$ is strictly concave on $[C_{\rm end},C_{\rm sat}]$. These assertions concern failure measures, not physical-label minima or whole-instrument uniqueness.

Proof. At each strict-interior budget there is a finite attained supporting certificate

$$
\operatorname{tr}H+\eta\lambda=\Phi(\lambda),\quad \eta\ge0,\quad H=H^T,
\qquad e^THe+\eta J(e_2^2)\ge(e^T\Xi e)_+
\quad(\|e\|=1).
\tag{TC.5}
$$

For completeness, strict feasibility and separation justify finite attainment here. The critical covariance measure has unit whitened masses on the two lines with $p=1/2$ and opposite cross signs. Mix it with a sufficiently small positive amount of uniform circle covariance measure. The cost is still strictly below $\lambda$ and a positive uniform density component remains. Perturb that component by small independent $\cos2t$ and $\sin2t$ densities. Both independent trace-fixed covariance coordinates vary over a neighborhood while positivity and the strict cost margin persist. The convex hull of the compact circle moment/objective image is compact at fixed mass two; adjoining upward cost slack and downward objective slack is locally closed. Separation at the maximum therefore has nonzero objective coefficient: a zero coefficient would support the covariance/cost projection at its interior point. Normalize that coefficient to one. Upward cost slack forces $\eta\ge0$; the two covariance multipliers and the constant multiplier combine as the symmetric $H$. This proves (TC.5) and equality of its value, rather than assuming an infinite-dimensional dual minimizer. Its integral nonnegative gap gives complementarity for every optimizing measure.

Cost is active by JS's strict increase. Moreover $\eta>0$. Otherwise the inequality says both $H\succeq0$ and $H\succeq\Xi$. Pairing with the positive spectral projection and its complement gives $\operatorname{tr}H\ge\gamma$, contradicting $\Phi(\lambda)<\gamma$.

Write $H=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}a&c\\c&b\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ and $\ell=b-a$. The full-circle inequality (TC.5) is equivalent to TWO WHOLE-INTERVAL inequalities

$$
\begin{aligned}
\psi_0(t)&=\eta J(t)+a+\ell t-2|c|v(t)\ge0,\\
\psi_1(t)&=\eta J(t)+a+d_0+(\ell-d_0)t-2|c+\zeta|v(t)\ge0,
\qquad 0\le t\le1.
\end{aligned}
\tag{TC.6}
$$

Indeed they minimize, over both cross signs, the inequalities against zero and against $e^T\Xi e$ respectively. Continuity includes both axes and all sectors. Each function is strictly convex because $\eta>0$, $J$ is strictly convex and $-v$ is convex. A nonnegative strictly convex function has at most one zero: two distinct zeros would give a negative interior value.

The optimal positive objective and the fixed signed expectation $\int e^T\Xi e\,dm=-d_0<0$ require both positive and negative contacts. A positive contact has cross sign $-$ and coordinate $p\in(p_z,1)$; it is the unique zero of $\psi_1$. A zero-expectation contact would zero both inequalities, hence have this same $p$. At this $p$ the cross-minus line is strictly positive and the cross-plus line strictly negative. Neither is zero, a contradiction. This also excludes zero-expectation axis contacts.

At the positive contact $c+\zeta>0$. A negative sign would select the wrong cross sign; if it were zero, equality against $\Xi$ would also hold on the strictly negative opposite-sign line and violate the inequality against zero. All negative contacts use the unique zero $q$ of $\psi_0$. Covariance must cancel the positive contact's strictly negative cross entry, so a negative contact has positive cross entry and $q$ is not an axis. If $c>0$, every minimizing cross sign in $\psi_0$ would instead be negative. Therefore $c\le0$. The mass and $p$ moments force $q<1/2$: some positive mass lies above $1/2$ and all other mass has coordinate $q$. Thus

$$
0<q<\tfrac12<p_z<p<1.
$$

If $c<0$, only the cross-plus negative line is possible. The two weighted unit columns have product with their transpose $I_2$, hence form an orthogonal matrix. Both masses equal one and $q=1-p$.

If $c=0$, both cross signs at $q$ are permitted. Let $m$ be the whitened mass on the positive line. Total mass, $p$ moment and cross cancellation give

$$
\begin{gathered}
m=\frac{1-2q}{p-q},\\
\omega_+=\frac{2-m+m v(p)/v(q)}2,\qquad
\omega_-=\frac{2-m-m v(p)/v(q)}2.
\end{gathered}
\tag{TC.7}
$$

Here $\omega_+$ and $\omega_-$ belong to cross signs $+$ and $-$ on the negative lines. One has $0<m<2$, and

$$
(2-m)^2q(1-q)-m^2p(1-p)=1-m.
$$

Thus nonnegative masses are equivalent to $m\le1$. For $m<1$ there are exactly three positive masses; for $m=1$ the cross-minus negative mass vanishes and the pair is orthogonal. This proves the contact bound even for arbitrary supported measures.

Fix this one dual. Every optimizer is supported on the same at-most-three lines, since the zero coordinates and allowed signs just derived concern the certificate itself. Three distinct unit rank-one projectors in dimension two are linearly independent: their trace-one Bloch coordinates lie on a circle, and three distinct circle points are not collinear. The corresponding assertion for two is immediate. Covariance therefore fixes their weights uniquely, including a possible zero weight. This proves the stated line-measure uniqueness.

Finally, if $\Phi$ were affine on a nontrivial interval, fix a dual at a strict-interior point of that interval. Its supporting line agrees with $\Phi$ at two distinct strict-interior budgets. Optimizers at both budgets saturate that same certificate and therefore are the same line measure by the preceding argument. Active cost would make their budgets equal, a contradiction. Any affine interval touching an endpoint contains two such interior budgets. Concavity is already supplied by covariance-preserving mixtures, so its strengthening is strict throughout the closed interval. $\square$

